  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A sprawling area of high pressure will reside over \nmuch of the Eastern U.S. through the upcoming week. Beneath the \nhigh, cool low-level thicknesses and mostly clear skies should \nsupport great radiational cooling conditions. Blended guidance \nstruggles to capture lows in good radiational setups like this, and \nlows were dropped below blended guidance over the next few nights to \naccount for this. The coldest nights look to be Monday night and \nTuesday night. Despite many areas seeing frost/freeze impacts, the \nprogram has been ended for much of ENC due to the widespread freeze \nthat occurred this morning. In light of that, any frost/freeze \nheadlines will be confined to Downeast Carteret County, Pamlico \nCounty, Hyde County, and coastal Dare County. For those areas, it \nlooks like there will be just enough wind to prevent widespread \nfrost formation tonight. Lighter winds are expected Monday night and \nTuesday night, though, and frost headlines may be needed for those \nnights where the frost/freeze program is still ongoing. \n\nLow-level thicknesses will steadily warm mid to late week, and even \nthough high pressure will remain in place, highs and lows each day \nshould respond by trending up. By Thursday and Friday, 70s should \nmake a return appearance, especially away from the OBX. For the OBX, \nhighs will likely be tempered by a northeast or east flow off the \ncooler waters of the Labrador Current. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A very dry airmass remaining in place over the \ncoming week will exacerbate the worsening drought conditions across \nENC. Fortunately winds are not expected to be strong with the high \nnearby. However, fire weather concerns may grow with the curing \nvegetation combined with low RH values each afternoon. Highs will \nwarm back to above climo in the upper 60s to low 70s mid to late \nweek, but overnight lows will continue quite chilly and below\nclimo.",
